From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: wire PCIe on COMB1 out to the FPC connector

The last piece of the PCIe bring-up: the controller node, the COMB1 PHY node, and the one
board fact that decides whether any of it works.

THE PINS ARE THE POINT

The vendor boot chain declares the PCIe control GPIOs on bank PE. This board
routes them on bank PD -- PD20 PCIE_PWR_EN, PD21 PCIE-WAKEn, PD22 PCIE-PERSTn,
PD23 PCIE-CLKREQn (schematic p7 L115-121, p14 L64-68). PE11-14 only offer those
functions in their mux tables; they carry other nets here.

Measured at the U-Boot prompt on the 6 GiB board, 2026-09-21: with the PE pins the link
never leaves Detect (LTSSM 0x00, link status 0x1011, DLL down). With PD20 driven
and PERST cycled on PD22 it trains to Gen3 x1 with the Data Link Layer active
and the drive enumerates. Both pins are needed; PD20 alone changes nothing.

PD20 is modelled as a fixed regulator rather than a bare GPIO, because that is
what it is. This PCB has no 3.3 V slot rail and no load switch: J3 feeds an
adapter card carrying its own regulator, and PD20 is that regulator's enable.
As vpcie3v3-supply the ordering against PERST belongs to the framework instead
of to driver code that has to remember it.

THE WINDOWS

  dbi     0x06000000  measured; the UM prints the module base one window up
  app     0x06400000  the User Defined Space, dbi + 0x400000, where the LTSSM
                      control and the link status live
  config  0x20000000  an ordinary outbound region inside PCIE_SLAVE, reached
                      through the standard DesignWare iATU

PCIE_SLAVE's low range is 0x20000000..0x27FFFFFF. Config takes the first 2 MiB,
I/O the 16 MiB at 0x21000000 and memory the remaining 96 MiB, which ends exactly
on the window boundary -- no range reaches past what the memory map allows.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE

 - No interrupt-map and no legacy INTx controller. NVMe is an MSI device and the
   DWC MSI receiver is wired (SPI 153). INTx arrives through the SII interrupt
   and can be added when something needs it; a mapping nobody exercises is a
   mapping nobody has tested.
 - CLKREQ# on PD23 is left alone. The controller does not use it and L1
   substates are their own measurement.

The COMB1 node maps three windows, the middle of which is shared with combophy0
-- both protocol muxes live in that page. The PHY driver maps it without
claiming it, for reasons its own header explains.

Verified so far: the dtb builds, and every phandle resolves (compile-check
builds it as part of the gate). The hardware proof is a booted kernel that
enumerates the drive; this
header does not claim it.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -594,6 +594,27 @@
 	 * Deliberately NOT regulator-always-on: the HUSB311 owns this rail as its
 	 * vbus-supply, and TCPM only leaves SRC_ATTACH_WAIT with VBUS down.
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * PCIE_PWR_EN on PD20. This does NOT switch a rail on this PCB: the
+	 * board has no 3.3 V slot rail and no load switch. The FPC connector J3
+	 * feeds an adapter card that carries its own regulator, and this pin is
+	 * that regulator's enable (schematic p7 L115, p14 L64). Modelled as a
+	 * fixed regulator so the controller can hold it as vpcie3v3-supply and
+	 * the ordering against PERST is the framework's job, not a driver's.
+	 *
+	 * Measured: with this pin low the link never leaves Detect, because the
+	 * device on the far end is simply unpowered.
+	 */
+	reg_pcie_pwr: regulator-pcie-pwr {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "vcc3v3-pcie";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		gpio = <&pio 3 20 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;	/* PD20 */
+		enable-active-high;
+		startup-delay-us = <20000>;
+	};
+
 	reg_usb3_vbus: regulator-usb3-vbus {
 		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
 		regulator-name = "usb3-vbus";
@@ -1965,6 +1986,85 @@
 		 * The old note here said "IRQ 185" and mixed the two numbering
 		 * spaces: INTID 185 is PCIE0_MSI.
 		 */
+		/*
+		 * PCIe root complex. The DBI is at 0x06000000 -- measured, not
+		 * where the UM prints the module base: that address (0x06400000)
+		 * is the User Defined Space, dbi + 0x400000, which is where the
+		 * LTSSM control and link status this driver uses live. The
+		 * config window is an ordinary outbound region inside
+		 * PCIE_SLAVE, reached through the standard DesignWare iATU.
+		 */
+		pcie: pcie@6000000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-pcie";
+			reg = <0x0 0x06000000 0x0 0x400000>,
+			      <0x0 0x06400000 0x0 0x80000>,
+			      <0x0 0x20000000 0x0 0x200000>;
+			reg-names = "dbi", "app", "config";
+			device_type = "pci";
+			#address-cells = <3>;
+			#size-cells = <2>;
+			bus-range = <0x00 0xff>;
+			num-lanes = <1>;
+			max-link-speed = <3>;
+			linux,pci-domain = <0>;
+			/*
+			 * PCIE_SLAVE low range is 0x20000000..0x27FFFFFF. Config
+			 * takes the first 2 MiB, I/O the 16 MiB at 0x21000000,
+			 * and memory the remaining 96 MiB, which ends exactly on
+			 * the window boundary.
+			 */
+			ranges = <0x81000000 0x0 0x21000000 0x0 0x21000000 0x0 0x01000000>,
+				 <0x82000000 0x0 0x22000000 0x0 0x22000000 0x0 0x06000000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 153 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupt-names = "msi";
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_PCIE_AUX>,
+				 <&ccu CLK_PCIE_AXI_SLV>,
+				 <&ccu CLK_BUS_ITS_PCIE0_ACLK>,
+				 <&serdes_ccu CLK_PCIE_SLV_ACLK>,
+				 <&serdes_ccu CLK_PCIE_MST_ACLK>,
+				 <&serdes_ccu CLK_PCIE_HCLK>;
+			clock-names = "aux", "axi-slv", "its",
+				      "slv-aclk", "mst-aclk", "hclk";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_PCIE_PWRUP>,
+				 <&ccu RST_BUS_PCIE>,
+				 <&ccu RST_BUS_ITS_PCIE0>;
+			reset-names = "pwrup", "core", "its";
+			phys = <&combophy1>;
+			phy-names = "pcie-phy";
+			/*
+			 * PERST# on PD22 (schematic p7 L119). The controller has
+			 * an internal PERST generator in SUBSYS_PCIE_BGR bit1,
+			 * but it is not wired to the connector on this board --
+			 * that bit reads 0 while a link is trained.
+			 */
+			reset-gpios = <&pio 3 22 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			vpcie3v3-supply = <&reg_pcie_pwr>;
+			power-domains = <&pck600 PD_PCIE>;
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * COMB1, the second combo PHY. It carries either PCIe or a
+		 * second USB3 SuperSpeed link; this board routes PCIe out of it
+		 * to J3, and the driver writes the protocol mux accordingly.
+		 *
+		 * The middle window is shared with combophy0 above -- both muxes
+		 * live in that page -- so the driver maps it without claiming it.
+		 */
+		combophy1: phy@6c02000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-pcie-combophy";
+			reg = <0x0 0x06c02000 0x0 0xa00>,
+			      <0x0 0x06c06000 0x0 0x1000>,
+			      <0x0 0x06ca0000 0x0 0x20000>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_SERDES_PHY_CFG>,
+				 <&serdes_ccu CLK_COMBPHY1_REFCLK>;
+			clock-names = "serdes", "refclk";
+			/* shared: combophy0 and the serdes_ccu hold it too */
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_SERDES>;
+			#phy-cells = <0>;
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
 		usb3: usb@6a00000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3";
 			reg = <0x0 0x06a00000 0x0 0x10000>;
